// Lexical path manipulation. Platform-aware separators, no filesystem.
//
// ---- A path is a `Path` ----
//
// It was a `str` until 2026-08-05, and the argument for that is worth keeping rather than
// deleting, because two thirds of it were right and the reversal turns on the third.
//
// The argument was: (1) every `std::string` operation on a path is meaningful, so wrapping
// it makes the useful operations the ones you cast away from; (2) the real bugs are wrong
// VALUES -- an unnormalised `..`, a joined absolute silently replacing its base, a prefix
// check done on bytes instead of components -- and a nominal wrapper catches none of them;
// (3) `std::fs` takes `str`, so a `Path` this module could not force on `fs` would mean a
// cast at every call site, which is friction bought with nothing.
//
// (3) was the load-bearing one, and it is now false: `std::fs` takes `Path`. The two modules
// changed together, which is the only way this was ever going to be worth doing.
//
// (1) does not survive contact. `to_upper` breaks a case-sensitive path, `replace` corrupts
// separators, and `concat` where `join` was meant is the oldest path bug there is. The list
// in (2) even names one of them -- "a prefix check done on bytes instead of components" is a
// string operation being wrong for a path, offered as evidence that a type would not help.
//
// (2) still stands, and is worth keeping in view: the type does not fix `..`. What it buys
// is a single door. `of` is the only way in, so normalisation and (later) validation have
// somewhere to live; `join` is the only way to combine two paths; and `to_str` makes leaving
// the API a visible act rather than the default.
//
// ---- No syscalls ----
//
// Everything here is a pure function of the string. There is no `exists`, no
// `canonicalize`, no `cwd`, and `normalize` resolves `..` *lexically* -- it does not read
// the filesystem, so it cannot know that a component is a symlink, and on a path containing
// symlinks the normalised result may name a different file than the original. Where that
// matters you want a real `realpath`, which needs a syscall and is not this module.
//
// ---- Platform rules ----
//
// The separator, what counts as a root, and how a path splits are the platform's business
// and live in the `posix` and `win` rule sets below. Everything else here is derived from
// them through `components` and `raw::render`, so the shared logic is genuinely shared
// rather than duplicated per platform.
//
// BOTH rule sets are compiled on every platform and both are tested on every platform; only
// which one the module uses is a `@cfg` question. That is deliberate: a `@cfg`-guarded branch
// is dropped before the checker runs, so rules written behind `@cfg(windows)` would never be
// type-checked anywhere but Windows and would ship compiled by nobody. Choosing is the one
// job `@cfg` can do without hiding code from the checker, and the four delegators in `plat`
// are all it does. `neon check --cfg windows` covers those.
//
// What the Windows rules DO cover: `\` and `/` both separate, `C:\` and `\\server` are
// roots, and a drive-relative `C:x` or `\x` is correctly not absolute. What they do not:
// case-insensitive comparison, `\\?\` extended-length prefixes, and reserved device names
// (`CON`, `NUL`). Those are real and unimplemented rather than overlooked.
//
// Bytes, not characters: everything is byte-oriented like `std::string`, which is fine
// because the bytes with meaning are ASCII and UTF-8 never produces them as continuations.
//
// Bytes, not characters: everything is byte-oriented like `std::string`, which is fine
// because the only byte with meaning is `/` and UTF-8 never produces it as a continuation.

use std::string;
use std::list;

// ---- platform rules ----

// Both rule sets are compiled on EVERY platform, and both are tested on every platform.
// Only which one the module uses is a `@cfg` question.
//
// That split is the whole design, and it is not stylistic. A `@cfg`-guarded branch is
// dropped BEFORE the checker runs, so a rule set written behind `@cfg` is never type-checked
// anywhere except the platform it is for -- which for this repo means the Windows rules would
// ship having been compiled by nobody. Keeping the rules always-compiled means Linux CI type
// checks them and `path_windows_rules.neon` runs them, and `@cfg` is left with the one job it
// can do safely: choosing.
//
// `neon check --cfg windows` additionally checks the delegators below, which ARE `@cfg`-ed
// because there is nothing else they could be.

mod posix {
    fn sep() -> str {
        "/"
    }

    /// The absolute prefix, or `null` when the path is relative. A leading `~` is a shell
    /// convention, not a path one.
    fn root_of(p: str) -> str | null {
        if string::starts_with(p, "/") { "/" } else { null }
    }

    fn split_parts(p: str) -> List[str] {
        string::split(p, "/")
    }

    fn is_root(part: str) -> bool {
        part == "/"
    }
}

mod win {
    fn sep() -> str {
        "\\"
    }

    /// `C:\x` is absolute and so is `\\server\share`. Deliberately NOT absolute: a bare
    /// `\x`, which is relative to the current drive, and `C:x`, which is relative to that
    /// drive's own working directory. Both name different files depending on state this
    /// module cannot see, and calling them absolute is how a join silently escapes its base.
    fn root_of(p: str) -> str | null {
        if string::starts_with(p, "\\\\") or string::starts_with(p, "//") {
            try! string::slice(p, 0, 2)
        } else if string::byte_len(p) >= 3 and is_drive_letter(try! string::slice(p, 0, 1)) {
            let rest = try! string::slice(p, 1, 3);
            if rest == ":\\" or rest == ":/" { try! string::slice(p, 0, 3) } else { null }
        } else {
            null
        }
    }

    /// Both separators, because every Windows API accepts `/` and real paths mix them.
    fn split_parts(p: str) -> List[str] {
        let out: List[str] = [];
        for chunk in string::split(p, "\\") {
            for part in string::split(chunk, "/") {
                out = list::push(out, part);
            };
        };
        out
    }

    /// A root component is a drive (`C:\`) or the UNC prefix, never a plain name.
    fn is_root(part: str) -> bool {
        part == "\\\\" or part == "//" or string::byte_len(part) == 3 and is_drive_letter(try! string::slice(part, 0, 1))
    }

    fn is_drive_letter(c: str) -> bool {
        let up = string::to_upper(c);
        up >= "A" and up <= "Z" and string::byte_len(up) == 1
    }
}

// ---- the shape of a path ----

/// The rest of the platform surface, selected the same way. Four one-line delegators rather
/// than a `@cfg` around the whole module: the logic below is shared, and only these four
/// facts differ.
@cfg(windows)
internal mod plat {
    fn sep() -> str {
        win::sep()
    }
    fn root_of(p: str) -> str | null {
        win::root_of(p)
    }
    fn split_parts(p: str) -> List[str] {
        win::split_parts(p)
    }
    fn is_root(part: str) -> bool {
        win::is_root(part)
    }
}

@cfg(not(windows))
internal mod plat {
    fn sep() -> str {
        posix::sep()
    }
    fn root_of(p: str) -> str | null {
        posix::root_of(p)
    }
    fn split_parts(p: str) -> List[str] {
        posix::split_parts(p)
    }
    fn is_root(part: str) -> bool {
        posix::is_root(part)
    }
}

// ---- Path ----

/// A path is its own type, opaque, wrapping the text.
///
/// This REVERSES the argument that stood here before, and the reversal has a reason rather
/// than a preference. That argument had three parts, and the load-bearing one was that
/// `std::fs` takes `str`, so a `Path` would mean an `as str` at every call site -- friction
/// bought with nothing. `std::fs` takes `Path` now, so that cost is gone.
///
/// The first part -- "every `std::string` operation on a path is meaningful" -- does not
/// survive contact either. `to_upper` breaks a case-sensitive path, `replace` corrupts
/// separators, and `concat` where `join` was meant is the classic path bug. This module's own
/// header already listed "a prefix check done on bytes instead of components" as a defect
/// worth designing against; that IS a string operation being wrong for a path.
///
/// The second part still stands and is worth keeping in view: the real bugs are wrong VALUES
/// -- an unnormalised `..`, a joined absolute silently replacing its base -- and a wrapper
/// catches none of them by itself. What it does buy is a single door. `of` is the only way in,
/// so normalisation has somewhere to live, and `join` is the only way to combine two paths.
///
/// Opaque rather than `newtype`: a newtype would let any `str` be cast to a `Path` and back,
/// which is the boundary being drawn. `of` is deliberately still total -- a path comes from
/// user input and the environment, and refusing to build one is not this module's call.
opaque record Path { s: str }

/// The only constructor. Total: any text names something on some platform, and validating
/// here would reject paths the OS accepts.
fn of(s: str) -> Path {
    Path { s: s }
}

/// The text back. The escape hatch is deliberate and named: code that has to hand a path to
/// something outside this API -- a C interface, a log line, a protocol -- should be visible.
fn to_str(p: Path) -> str {
    p.s
}

/// So a path interpolates like anything else: `"reading #{p}"`.
impl Display for Path {
    fn to_string(v: Path) -> str {
        v.s
    }
}

/// Absolute iff it starts at a root. Which prefixes count is the platform's business; see the
/// rule sets above.
fn is_absolute(p: Path) -> bool {
    !plat::root_of(p.s) is null
}

/// The negation of `is_absolute`: a path that resolves against some other directory rather
/// than at a root.
fn is_relative(p: Path) -> bool {
    !is_absolute(p)
}

/// The meaningful parts of `p`, left to right, and the primitive the rest of this module is
/// built on. An absolute path gets `"/"` as its first component; nothing else can produce a
/// `"/"` component, so the root is distinguishable from a name.
///
/// Three things are dropped, because none of them changes which file a path names:
///
///   * empty components, so repeated separators collapse -- `a//b` is `["a", "b"]`
///   * a trailing separator -- `a/b/` is `["a", "b"]`, same as `a/b`
///   * `.` components -- `./a/./b` is `["a", "b"]`, and `.` alone is `[]`
///
/// `..` is *kept*. It is not resolved here: `/a/../b` is `["/", "a", "..", "b"]`. Resolving
/// it is `normalize`'s job, and keeping the two separate is what lets `starts_with` compare
/// paths exactly as written.
///
/// The empty string has no components. `components("")` and `components(".")` are both `[]`,
/// which is the honest answer: neither names anything.
fn components(p: Path) -> List[str] {
    let out: List[str] = [];
    let p = p.s;
    let root = plat::root_of(p);
    let rest = if root is null {
        p
    } else {
        let r = root as str;
        // In range: `root_of` returned a prefix of `p`.
        try! string::slice(p, string::byte_len(r), string::byte_len(p))
    };
    if !root is null {
        out = list::push(out, root as str);
    };
    for part in plat::split_parts(rest) {
        if !string::is_empty(part) and part != "." {
            out = list::push(out, part);
        };
    };
    out
}

internal mod raw {
    /// Components back to a string. The inverse of `components` up to the things
    /// `components` deliberately drops, so it round-trips a normalised path exactly.
    fn render(parts: List[str]) -> str {
        if list::is_empty(parts) {
            ""
        } else if plat::is_root(parts[0]) {
            string::concat(parts[0], string::join(drop_first(parts), plat::sep()))
        } else {
            string::join(parts, plat::sep())
        }
    }

    fn drop_first(xs: List[str]) -> List[str] {
        let out: List[str] = [];
        let i = 1;
        while i < list::len(xs) {
            out = list::push(out, xs[i]);
            i = i + 1;
        };
        out
    }

    fn drop_last(xs: List[str]) -> List[str] {
        let out: List[str] = [];
        let i = 0;
        let n = list::len(xs);
        while i < n - 1 {
            out = list::push(out, xs[i]);
            i = i + 1;
        };
        out
    }

    /// Strip every trailing separator. `"/"` becomes `""`; the caller decides what that
    /// means, because for `join` it means the root and for nothing else does.
    fn trim_seps(s: str) -> str {
        let out = s;
        while string::ends_with(out, plat::sep()) or string::ends_with(out, "/") {
            out = string::strip_suffix(out, plat::sep());
            out = string::strip_suffix(out, "/");
        };
        out
    }

    /// The byte offset of the dot that starts an extension, or `null`.
    ///
    /// The scan starts at 1 and stops before the last byte, which encodes both rules worth
    /// having: a *leading* dot is part of the name, so `.bashrc` has no extension and is not
    /// a nameless file with a `bashrc` one; and a *trailing* dot starts nothing, so `a.` has
    /// no extension rather than an empty one. Ruling out the empty extension is why nothing
    /// else in this module has to think about it.
    ///
    /// The last such dot wins: `a.tar.gz` is stem `a.tar`, extension `gz`.
    fn dot_at(name: str) -> i64 | null {
        let n = string::byte_len(name);
        let found: i64 | null = null;
        let i = 1;
        while i + 1 < n {
            if (try? string::char_at(name, i) orelse "") == "." {
                found = i;
            };
            i = i + 1;
        };
        found
    }
}

// ---- building ----

/// `b` resolved against `a`.
///
/// An absolute `b` *replaces* `a` entirely: `join("/etc", "/tmp/x")` is `/tmp/x`. This is
/// the one surprising rule here and it is deliberate -- it is what makes `join(base, arg)`
/// behave like passing `arg` to a command, where an absolute argument ignores the working
/// directory. Check `is_absolute` first if a caller must not escape `a`.
///
/// Separators are neither doubled nor required: `join("a/", "b")` and `join("a", "b")` are
/// both `a/b`. An empty operand contributes nothing, so `join("a", "")` is `a` and not
/// `a/` -- joining nothing on should not change what the path names.
fn join(a: Path, b: Path) -> Path {
    if is_absolute(b) {
        b
    } else if string::is_empty(b.s) {
        a
    } else if string::is_empty(a.s) {
        b
    } else {
        let base = raw::trim_seps(a.s);
        if string::is_empty(base) {
            // `a` was nothing but separators: that is the root.
            of(string::concat(plat::sep(), b.s))
        } else {
            of(string::concat(string::concat(base, plat::sep()), b.s))
        }
    }
}

/// Everything but the last component, or `null` when there is nothing left to name.
///
/// `null` rather than a throw, and rather than `""`: asking for the parent of a path is
/// ordinary, and running out of parents is an answer, not a failure. `parent("/")` is `null`
/// -- the root is its own parent on POSIX, and returning `"/"` forever would turn a
/// walk-upwards loop into a spin. `parent("a")` is also `null`: the empty string is not a
/// path any syscall accepts, and inventing `"."` would name a directory the caller never
/// wrote. Write `parent(p) orelse "."` if that is the meaning you want.
///
/// Trailing separators and `.` do not count as components, so `parent("a/b/")` is `a` and
/// `parent("a/./b")` is `a`. `..` does count, since it is not resolved: `parent("a/..")` is
/// `a`, not `null`.
fn parent(p: Path) -> Path | null {
    let parts = components(p);
    if list::len(parts) <= 1 {
        null
    } else {
        of(raw::render(raw::drop_last(parts)))
    }
}

/// The last component, when it is a name.
///
/// `null` when the path has no components (`""`, `"/"`, `"."`) and also when the last
/// component is `".."` -- `a/..` names a directory, but not by name, and answering `".."`
/// would hand back something no caller can use as a filename. Trailing separators are
/// ignored, so `file_name("a/b/")` is `b`.
fn file_name(p: Path) -> str | null {
    let last = list::last(components(p));
    if last is null {
        null
    } else {
        let name = last as str;
        if name == "/" or name == ".." {
            null
        } else {
            name
        }
    }
}

/// The file name without its extension, or `null` wherever `file_name` is `null`. A name
/// with no extension is its own stem: `stem("README")` is `README`, and `stem(".bashrc")` is
/// `.bashrc`.
fn stem(p: Path) -> str | null {
    let name = file_name(p);
    if name is null {
        null
    } else {
        let s = name as str;
        let at = raw::dot_at(s);
        if at is null {
            s
        } else {
            try? string::slice(s, 0, at as i64) orelse s
        }
    }
}

/// The extension without its dot, or `null` when there is none. Never `""`: see
/// `raw::dot_at` for why `a.` has no extension.
fn extension(p: Path) -> str | null {
    let name = file_name(p);
    if name is null {
        null
    } else {
        let s = name as str;
        let at = raw::dot_at(s);
        if at is null {
            null
        } else {
            try? string::slice(s, at as i64 + 1, string::byte_len(s))
        }
    }
}

/// `p` with its extension replaced by `ext`, which is written *without* a leading dot:
/// `with_extension("a/b.txt", "md")` is `a/b.md`. An empty `ext` removes the extension
/// instead of leaving a trailing dot, so `with_extension("a/b.txt", "")` is `a/b`.
///
/// `null` when there is no file name to attach an extension to -- `"/"`, `""`, `"a/.."`.
/// The alternative was returning `p` unchanged, which silently reports success for an
/// operation that did not happen; a caller that genuinely does not care can write
/// `with_extension(p, e) orelse p` and say so.
fn with_extension(p: Path, ext: str) -> Path | null {
    let base = stem(p);
    if base is null {
        null
    } else {
        let b = base as str;
        let name = if string::is_empty(ext) { b } else { "#{b}.#{ext}" };
        of(raw::render(list::push(raw::drop_last(components(p)), name)))
    }
}

// ---- normalising ----

/// `p` with `.`, empty and repeated separators removed and `..` resolved against the
/// components to its left. Purely lexical: nothing here touches the filesystem, so a `..`
/// that crosses a symlink resolves to the wrong place. See the header.
///
/// The two cases worth stating:
///
///   * `..` above the root is the root. `/..` and `/a/../..` are both `/`. This is not a
///     fudge -- POSIX defines `..` in `/` as `/` -- so the result names the same directory
///     the kernel would reach.
///   * `..` above a *relative* path is kept. `../a` stays `../a` and `a/../..` becomes `..`,
///     because without a working directory -- which needs a syscall -- there is nothing to
///     resolve them against, and dropping them would silently change which file is named.
///
/// A relative path that normalises to nothing becomes `"."`, not `""`: `normalize("a/..")`,
/// `normalize(".")` and `normalize("")` are all `"."`. This is the one place the module
/// invents a component, and it does so because `normalize`'s whole job is to hand back a
/// string that can be used as a path -- and `""` cannot be.
fn normalize(p: Path) -> Path {
    let abs = is_absolute(p);
    let out: List[str] = [];
    for part in components(p) {
        if part == ".." {
            let top = list::last(out);
            if top is null {
                // Nothing to pop. At the root `..` is the root, so it vanishes; relative,
                // it has to survive.
                if !abs {
                    out = list::push(out, "..");
                };
            } else if top as str == ".." {
                // Only reachable when relative: leading `..`s stack.
                out = list::push(out, "..");
            } else {
                out = raw::drop_last(out);
            };
        } else if !plat::is_root(part) {
            out = list::push(out, part);
        };
    };
    if abs {
        // The root the path actually had, not a hardcoded `/`: on Windows it is the drive.
        let root = plat::root_of(p.s) orelse plat::sep();
        of(string::concat(root, string::join(out, plat::sep())))
    } else if list::is_empty(out) {
        of(".")
    } else {
        of(string::join(out, plat::sep()))
    }
}

/// Is `prefix` a leading run of whole components of `p`?
///
/// Component-wise, not byte-wise, which is the entire reason this exists rather than
/// `string::starts_with`: `/foobar` does *not* start with `/foo`, though its bytes do. That
/// distinction is the difference between a containment check and a sandbox escape.
///
/// Compared as written -- `..` is not resolved on either side, so normalize both first if
/// they may not be in the same form. The empty prefix is a prefix of everything, since it
/// has no components to fail to match. An absolute prefix only matches an absolute path,
/// because `"/"` is a component that only absolute paths have.
fn starts_with(p: Path, prefix: Path) -> bool {
    let a = components(p);
    let b = components(prefix);
    if list::len(b) > list::len(a) {
        false
    } else {
        let ok = true;
        let i = 0;
        while i < list::len(b) {
            if a[i] != b[i] {
                ok = false;
            };
            i = i + 1;
        };
        ok
    }
}
